-- title:
--   Equivalence invariance of the ordinary condition
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a commutative ring and $A$ a commutative local ring that is an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra, let $p$ be a natural number, let $S$ be a finite set of natural numbers, and let $\rho,\rho'$ be two objects of [`GaloisRepAdic A`](def/GaloisRep_Adic.html#L16), that is, each consists of a finite free $A$-module $V$ of rank $2$ together with a monoid homomorphism from $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ (the automorphism group of `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` over $\mathbb{Q}$) to $\mathrm{End}_A(V)$ which is adically continuous in the sense that for every $n$ there is a finite extension $L/\mathbb{Q}$ inside $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ such that every $\sigma$ fixing $L$ pointwise satisfies $\rho(\sigma)v-v\in\mathfrak{m}_A^n\cdot V$ for all $v$. Assume $\rho$ and $\rho'$ are equivalent, i.e. there exists an $A$-linear isomorphism of their underlying modules intertwining the two Galois actions. The conclusion is an implication: if $\rho$ satisfies [`GaloisRep.ordinaryCondition 𝒪 p S`](def/GaloisRep_LocalConditions.html#L28), then so does $\rho'$. That condition is the conjunction of three clauses: (i) $p\in\mathfrak{m}_A$ and, for all $n$, $\sigma$ and $a$ with $\sigma\mu=\mu^{a}$ for every $p^n$-th root of unity $\mu$, one has $\det\rho(\sigma)\equiv a$ modulo the ideal generated by $p^{n}$; (ii) for every valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $p$ there is an $A$-submodule $L\subseteq V$ spanned by the first vector of some basis indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,2$, stable under the decomposition subgroup, with $\rho(\sigma)v-v\in L$ for all $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup and all $v\in V$; (iii) for every prime $q\notin S$ and every valuation subring over $q$, the inertia subgroup acts trivially.
--
--   This records that the ordinary local condition, as a condition on two-dimensional adic Galois representations with coefficients in a local $\mathcal{O}$-algebra, depends only on the equivalence class of the representation, as a deformation condition must. It is used when assembling patching data for residually modular representations, where conditions have to be transported between equivalent Hecke–Galois representations.
